{
  "reviews": [
    {
      "benchmark": "arc",
      "instrument_review": "This passage-based MESA review supports using ARC-AGI-2 to compare performance on unfamiliar grid puzzles under specified conditions. Development studies, public marking tests and verified-testing rules support inspection and interpretation. Evidence remains incomplete for score stability, comparisons across revisions and broader reasoning claims. Testing is credited where reported, without assuming favourable results.",
      "supported_claims": [
        "The evaluated version and scoring method are identified: public ARC-AGI-2 problems and official software average scores within and across problems.",
        "The tasks assess inferring grid transformations from examples: outputs must match the required dimensions and colours.",
        "Human solving informed development and test-set matching, although the study pool exceeded the final test.",
        "Marking received software tests covering correct outputs, wrong dimensions, changed squares and missing outputs.",
        "Verified evaluations specify settings, spending limits and restrictions on using feedback for development."
      ],
      "documented_problems": [
        "Public problems permit prior exposure; hidden-data safeguards acknowledge possible leakage.",
        "Corrections addressed coloured squares and unclear examples, but affected AI scores were not all recalculated.",
        "Scoring allows partial problem credit, while the dataset's 'fully solved' description requires every input to be correct.",
        "Testing routes can use different conditions, limiting direct comparisons across routes."
      ],
      "missing_information": [
        "Repeated identical AI runs and score-variation estimates are not supplied.",
        "Systematic tests of instruction, presentation and software changes are not supplied.",
        "Evidence connecting grid scores to language, social interaction or real-world performance is absent.",
        "The effects of revisions on all affected scores and equivalence across benchmark versions are not established.",
        "The passage does not establish that all unintended routes to high scores have been investigated."
      ],
      "reinterpretations": [
        "AI refers to the evaluated system under its settings, resources and permitted attempts; results need not describe an underlying model alone.",
        "Human difficulty matching concerns people solving puzzles and does not establish equal difficulty or stability for AI systems.",
        "A problem score is the fraction of new inputs solved; it is not necessarily the fraction of whole problems fully solved."
      ],
      "closing_note": "No separate review method is assigned, so MESA is applied directly. Questions combining several requirements can warrant Partly even when some evidence is clear; missing studies are treated as Not sure.",
      "mesa_answers": [
        {
          "id": 1,
